= Feedback =

== Overview ==
'''Feedback''' occurs when information about a system's output or state is returned into the system and influences subsequent behavior.

Feedback is central to control theory, cybernetics, biology, engineering, and many complex systems.

== Closed loop ==
A feedback structure can be represented as:

<pre>
system → output/state
   ↑         ↓
   └── feedback ──
</pre>

The returned signal affects later inputs, control actions, or internal state.

== Negative feedback ==
Negative feedback tends to oppose deviations from a reference, equilibrium, or target condition.

It can stabilize systems, though delay or excessive gain can also produce oscillation or instability.

“Negative” describes the loop effect, not whether the result is bad.

== Positive feedback ==
Positive feedback reinforces deviations or changes.

It can generate growth, amplification, cascades, tipping behavior, or rapid transitions.

“Positive” does not mean beneficial.

== Feedback and response ==
A system responding to an input is not automatically feedback.

Feedback specifically requires that consequences of prior system behavior influence later behavior through a loop.

== Related concepts ==
[[System boundary]], [[State machine]], [[Complex system]], [[Self-organization]], [[Adaptation (systems)]], [[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]], and [[Distributed system]] provide neighboring system concepts.

== Biological and ecological feedback ==
[[Related to::Homeostasis|Homeostatic regulation]] can use negative feedback to counter perturbations in physiological variables, but an unchanged variable alone does not identify a feedback loop. In [[Related to::Ecosystem|ecosystem models]], a change in one population may affect food availability or predation and subsequently alter that population's future growth. Whether such an effect is reinforcing or stabilizing depends on interaction signs, delays and the full model.

== Evidence and limits ==
A feedback claim needs a causal loop linking an effect back to a later cause; correlations, food-web arrows and coincident trends are not sufficient. Dynamic observations or interventions can help test a proposed loop. Under some gains and delays, even nominally negative feedback can oscillate or destabilize the modeled system.

